from gklib import L, S, Q
L("history","river-valley-civilisations","11-13","The First Great Civilisations","How life along great rivers led to cities, writing and laws in Mesopotamia, Egypt, the Indus Valley and China.","🏺",[
 S("Why rivers?","Long ago people moved from hunting and gathering to farming. | Rivers gave water, rich soil and an easy way to travel. | So the first big cities grew beside rivers.",["Farming began","Rivers: water, soil, travel"]),
 S("Four river valleys","Mesopotamia grew between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, in what is now Iraq. | Egypt grew along the Nile, the Indus Valley civilisation along the Indus and nearby rivers, and ancient China along the Yellow River. | These all began roughly between five thousand and four thousand years ago.",["Mesopotamia: Tigris and Euphrates","Egypt: the Nile","Indus Valley: the Indus","China: Yellow River"]),
 S("What they invented","Mesopotamians made one of the first writing systems, called cuneiform, pressed into clay. | Egyptians built pyramids and used a calendar based on the Nile's floods. | The Indus cities such as Mohenjo-daro and Harappa had straight streets, brick houses and drains.",["Cuneiform writing","Egypt's pyramids and calendar","Indus: planned cities and drains"]),
 S("Still to learn","We can read Egyptian and Mesopotamian writing, but nobody has fully read the Indus script yet. | So much about how the Indus people lived is learned from objects they left behind. | Historians and archaeologists keep finding new clues.",["Indus script is not yet read","Clues come from objects"]),
],facts=["Harappan cities used standard-sized bricks and had covered drains over four thousand years ago.","The Great Pyramid of Giza was the tallest human-made structure in the world for about 3,800 years.","The Indus Valley people used carefully made weights for trade."],
quiz=[Q("Why did early cities grow beside rivers?",["Rivers were pretty","Water, rich soil and easy travel","Mountains were cold"],1,"Farming needs water and soil, and rivers helped travel and trade."),
Q("Which river is linked to ancient Egypt?",["Nile","Indus","Yellow River"],0,"Ancient Egypt grew along the Nile."),
Q("What is true about the Indus script?",["It is fully read","It has not been fully read yet","It was never used"],1,"Scholars are still trying to understand the Indus script.")],tags=["civilisation","indus","egypt"],notes="Dates are approximate and given as round numbers.")

L("history","how-writing-and-printing-changed-the-world","11-13","From Clay Tablets to Printing","The story of how writing and printing helped ideas travel.","📜",[
 S("Before writing","Before writing, people passed on stories by speaking and remembering them. | Memory is powerful, but a story can change each time it is told. | Writing lets people store information exactly and share it across time.",["Spoken stories can change","Writing stores things exactly"]),
 S("Early writing","The Sumerians pressed signs into wet clay about five thousand years ago. | Egyptians used picture writing called hieroglyphs, often on papyrus made from reeds. | In India people wrote on palm leaves, and Tamil literature was preserved on palm leaf manuscripts for centuries.",["Clay tablets","Hieroglyphs on papyrus","Palm leaf manuscripts"]),
 S("Paper and printing","Paper was invented in China about two thousand years ago. | Printing with carved blocks began in China, and in the 1400s Johannes Gutenberg in Europe made printing with movable metal letters practical. | Books became cheaper and many more people could learn to read.",["Paper from China","Gutenberg's printing press","Cheaper books"]),
 S("Today","Now we can send writing around the world in a second. | The ideas are the same: store what we know and pass it on. | Every book you open is part of that long chain.",["Same idea, faster tools"]),
],facts=["Gutenberg's press in the mid 1400s let a printer make many copies far faster than copying by hand.","The Tamil word for a palm leaf manuscript is 'olai chuvadi'.","Some of the oldest known writing is on clay tablets from the city of Uruk."],
quiz=[Q("Which material did the Sumerians write on?",["Clay","Plastic","Cloth"],0,"They pressed signs into wet clay."),
Q("Who made the movable-type printing press practical in Europe?",["Gutenberg","Newton","Ashoka"],0,"Johannes Gutenberg did, in the 1400s."),
Q("In India, palm leaf manuscripts were used to write...",["Only maths","Many texts, including Tamil literature","Only maps"],1,"Palm leaves were used to preserve many works, including Tamil literature.")],tags=["writing","printing","books"])

L("history","ancient-india-in-brief","8-10","Ancient India in Brief","A fast journey through the Indus cities, the great kingdoms and the gifts of ancient Indian scholars.","🛕",[
 S("The first cities","About four and a half thousand years ago, cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro grew in the Indus Valley. | They had planned streets, drains and busy trade. | Later, other kingdoms grew along the Ganga plains and in the south.",["Indus cities","Later kingdoms grew"]),
 S("Great rulers","Ashoka of the Maurya dynasty ruled much of India more than two thousand years ago. | The Cholas of Tamil Nadu ruled for centuries and built the great temple at Thanjavur, completed around the year 1010. | Their ships sailed across the sea, and they traded with faraway lands.",["Ashoka, Maurya","Cholas built the Thanjavur temple"]),
 S("Learning and ideas","Indian scholars helped develop the number system we use today, including the idea of zero as a number. | Aryabhata wrote about astronomy and maths in the year 499. | Sushruta wrote about surgery, and Charaka about medicine, in ancient times.",["Zero and our number system","Aryabhata: astronomy","Sushruta and Charaka: medicine"]),
 S("Why it matters","The ideas we use every day, like our digits, came to the rest of the world through many peoples. | Learning how they spread shows that knowledge belongs to everyone. | History is the story of people borrowing, adding and sharing ideas.",["Ideas travel","Knowledge is shared"]),
],facts=["The shape of the digits 0 to 9 we use today developed from Indian numerals and travelled to the West through Arab scholars.","Brihadisvara Temple in Thanjavur has a tower about 66 metres tall.","The Ashoka Chakra on India's flag comes from the lion capital at Sarnath."],
quiz=[Q("Which ancient civilisation had cities like Harappa?",["Indus Valley","Roman","Mayan"],0,"Harappa was an Indus Valley city."),
Q("Which dynasty built the Brihadisvara Temple at Thanjavur?",["Maurya","Chola","Gupta"],1,"The Cholas built it around the year 1010."),
Q("Which idea did Indian mathematicians help develop?",["Zero as a number","The telephone","The steam engine"],0,"Indian mathematicians treated zero as a number and used it in calculation.")],tags=["india","ancient","chola"],notes="Years are as commonly taught; the temple was consecrated around 1010 CE.")

L("history","how-the-industrial-revolution-changed-life","14+","The Industrial Revolution","How steam, factories and railways changed work, cities and the world from the late 1700s.","🏭",[
 S("Before machines","Before the Industrial Revolution most people lived in villages and farmed. | Goods were made by hand in homes or small workshops. | Travel was as fast as a horse or a sailing ship.",["Farming and handwork","Slow travel"]),
 S("Steam power","In Britain in the 1700s, inventors improved the steam engine, and James Watt made it much more efficient. | Steam could drive machines in factories and pull trains on railways. | Cotton spinning and weaving machines made cloth far faster than hand work.",["Steam engines","Factories","Railways"]),
 S("Big changes","People moved from villages into growing cities to work in factories. | Many factories were crowded and unsafe, and children also worked, which led to new laws and trade unions. | Cities grew fast and also had pollution and poor housing for many years.",["Cities grew","Hard working conditions","New laws and unions"]),
 S("A world connected","Railways and steamships brought distant places closer and made trade faster. | The changes spread to Europe, America, and later Asia, including India, where railways were built from the 1850s. | Today's world of machines, mass production and energy use began here.",["Trade and transport sped up","Spread worldwide"]),
],facts=["The first passenger railway in India ran from Bombay to Thane in 1853.","Burning coal for factories began to raise the amount of carbon dioxide in the air.","Many historians say this change was as big as the move from hunting to farming."],
quiz=[Q("Which invention powered early factories and trains?",["Steam engine","Computer","Telescope"],0,"Improved steam engines drove machines and locomotives."),
Q("What happened to many cities?",["They shrank","They grew fast","They disappeared"],1,"People moved to cities to work in factories."),
Q("When was the first passenger train in India?",["1853","1953","1753"],0,"The first passenger train ran from Bombay to Thane in 1853.")],tags=["industrial","steam","history"])

L("india","indian-flag-and-national-symbols","5-7","India's Flag and National Symbols","Learn the colours of our flag and the symbols that stand for our country.","🇮🇳",[
 S("Our flag","The flag of India is called the Tiranga, which means tricolour. | It has three stripes: saffron at the top, white in the middle and green at the bottom. | In the middle of the white stripe is a blue wheel with 24 spokes, the Ashoka Chakra.",["Tricolour: three stripes","Saffron, white, green","Blue Ashoka Chakra with 24 spokes"]),
 S("What the colours mean","Saffron stands for courage and sacrifice. | White stands for peace and truth. | Green stands for faith, growth and the richness of the land.",["Saffron: courage","White: peace","Green: growth"]),
 S("National symbols","The national animal is the tiger, and the national bird is the peacock. | The national flower is the lotus, the national fruit is the mango, and the national tree is the banyan. | The national river is the Ganga and the national aquatic animal is the Ganges river dolphin.",["Tiger, peacock, lotus","Mango, banyan","Ganga"]),
 S("National song and anthem","Jana Gana Mana is our National Anthem, and it takes about 52 seconds to sing. | Vande Mataram is our National Song. | We stand up straight and show respect when the anthem is played.",["Jana Gana Mana: anthem","Vande Mataram: national song"]),
],facts=["The design of the flag was adopted on 22 July 1947, a few weeks before independence.","The Ashoka Chakra has 24 spokes, one for each hour of the day.","The Indian flag is traditionally made of khadi, hand-spun cloth."],
quiz=[Q("What is the colour at the top of our flag?",["Green","Saffron","White"],1,"The top stripe is saffron."),
Q("How many spokes does the Ashoka Chakra have?",["12","24","36"],1,"It has 24 spokes."),
Q("Which is India's national bird?",["Parrot","Peacock","Crow"],1,"The peacock is the national bird.")],tags=["flag","symbols","india"],notes="National symbols are as notified by the Government of India.")

L("india","the-indian-constitution-made-simple","11-13","The Constitution of India Made Simple","What the Constitution is, who wrote it and what rights and duties it gives every citizen.","📘",[
 S("The rule book","A constitution is the rule book of a country. | It says how the government works and what rights people have. | The Constitution of India came into force on 26 January 1950, which we celebrate as Republic Day.",["Rule book of the country","In force: 26 January 1950"]),
 S("Who wrote it?","A Constituent Assembly wrote it, and its work took nearly three years. | Dr B. R. Ambedkar was the chairman of the Drafting Committee, and Dr Rajendra Prasad was the president of the Assembly. | It is one of the longest written constitutions in the world.",["Constituent Assembly","Ambedkar: Drafting Committee","Rajendra Prasad: president"]),
 S("Big promises","The Preamble tells us what India promises: justice, liberty, equality and fraternity. | India is described as sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic and a republic. | Every citizen has the same rights, whatever their religion, caste, gender or birthplace.",["Justice, liberty, equality, fraternity","Everyone has equal rights"]),
 S("Rights and duties","Fundamental Rights include the right to equality, the right to freedom, and the right to education for children aged 6 to 14. | There are also Fundamental Duties, such as respecting the flag and caring for the environment. | Rights and duties go together: we enjoy our rights when everyone does their duty.",["Fundamental Rights","Fundamental Duties"]),
],facts=["The original Constitution was handwritten and decorated by artists, not printed.","India's Constitution has been amended many times to meet new needs.","26 November is observed as Constitution Day, because the Constitution was adopted on that day in 1949."],
quiz=[Q("When did the Constitution come into force?",["15 August 1947","26 January 1950","2 October 1948"],1,"It came into force on 26 January 1950, Republic Day."),
Q("Who chaired the Drafting Committee?",["Dr B. R. Ambedkar","Mahatma Gandhi","Jawaharlal Nehru"],0,"Dr B. R. Ambedkar chaired the Drafting Committee."),
Q("Which word is NOT in the list of Preamble promises?",["Justice","Liberty","Luck"],2,"The Preamble promises justice, liberty, equality and fraternity.")],tags=["constitution","rights","republic"],notes="Based on the text of the Constitution of India; details such as amendments can change over time.")

L("india","the-freedom-struggle-in-brief","11-13","India's Freedom Struggle in Brief","A short story of how Indians worked together to end British rule and win independence in 1947.","🕊️",[
 S("Before independence","For about two hundred years large parts of India were ruled by the British East India Company and then the British Crown. | Many Indians were unhappy because of heavy taxes, unfair laws and loss of freedom. | The uprising of 1857 was one of the first big revolts.",["British rule for about 200 years","1857 uprising"]),
 S("Many paths","In 1885 the Indian National Congress was formed. | Leaders like Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Lala Lajpat Rai and Bipin Chandra Pal asked for self-rule. | Revolutionaries like Bhagat Singh, Chandra Shekhar Azad and Subhas Chandra Bose also worked for freedom in their own ways.",["Congress formed in 1885","Many leaders, many methods"]),
 S("Gandhi and non-violence","Mahatma Gandhi led movements based on non-violence and truth, such as the Non-Cooperation Movement, the Salt March in 1930 and the Quit India Movement in 1942. | Millions of ordinary people took part, including women and young people. | Leaders from Tamil Nadu such as V. O. Chidambaram Pillai and Subramania Bharati also played big roles.",["Non-violence","Salt March, 1930","Quit India, 1942"]),
 S("Independence","India became independent on 15 August 1947. | The country was also divided, and many people suffered during the partition. | Jawaharlal Nehru became the first Prime Minister, and we remember the freedom fighters every year.",["15 August 1947","Partition was painful"]),
],facts=["The Dandi Salt March in 1930 covered about 390 kilometres on foot.","Subramania Bharati's songs inspired many people during the freedom struggle.","Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi and Velu Nachiyar of Sivaganga were among the brave women who fought British rule."],
quiz=[Q("When did India become independent?",["26 January 1950","15 August 1947","2 October 1869"],1,"India became independent on 15 August 1947."),
Q("Which march did Gandhi lead in 1930?",["Salt March","Silent March","Long March"],0,"The Salt March to Dandi was in 1930."),
Q("Which movement started in 1942?",["Quit India","Swadeshi","Non-Cooperation"],0,"The Quit India Movement was launched in 1942.")],tags=["freedom","gandhi","history"],notes="Condensed summary for young readers. Topics such as partition are described briefly and sensitively.")

L("india","states-and-capitals-made-memorable","8-10","Indian States and Capitals Made Memorable","Tricks to remember capitals and a quick tour of India's regions.","🗺️",[
 S("A big country","India has 28 states and 8 union territories. | The capital of India is New Delhi. | Each state has its own capital, language and traditions.",["28 states, 8 union territories","Capital: New Delhi"]),
 S("The south","Tamil Nadu's capital is Chennai, Kerala's is Thiruvananthapuram, and Karnataka's is Bengaluru. | Telangana's capital is Hyderabad. | Puducherry is a union territory in the south, and its capital has the same name.",["Tamil Nadu: Chennai","Kerala: Thiruvananthapuram","Karnataka: Bengaluru","Telangana: Hyderabad"]),
 S("Memory tricks","Link each place with a picture or a story you will remember. | Break long names into small parts, for example Thi-ru-va-nan-tha-pu-ram, and say them slowly. | Learn in groups: states that share a border, one region at a time, and draw a rough map as you go.",["Link names to pictures","Break long names into parts","Learn by region and draw a map"]),
 S("Languages and cultures","India has 22 languages listed in the Eighth Schedule of the Constitution, and hundreds more are spoken. | Each region has its own food, music, dance and festivals. | Learning about them is a good way to understand our unity in diversity.",["22 scheduled languages","Unity in diversity"]),
],facts=["The smallest Indian state by area is Goa.","The largest state by area is Rajasthan.","Telangana, formed in 2014, is the newest of India's states."],
quiz=[Q("What is the capital of Karnataka?",["Bengaluru","Mysuru","Hubli"],0,"Bengaluru is the capital of Karnataka."),
Q("What is the capital of India?",["Mumbai","New Delhi","Chennai"],1,"New Delhi is the capital."),
Q("Which state is the largest by area?",["Rajasthan","Goa","Kerala"],0,"Rajasthan is the largest state by area.")],tags=["states","capitals"],asof="November 2026",notes="The number of states and union territories can change if Parliament changes them. Check an official source for the latest.")
